WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Hi everyone, it's Jerry.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.360
I have an instructive game to share with you from the 2013 Candidates Tournament.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.760
On the white end, Magnus Carlsen playing against Boris Gelfand.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:23.040
We have a Sicilian Defense, Rossolimo Attack, e6, castles, and now if black questions the

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.800
bishop while this knight is not defended by a piece, white will take and saddle black

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.800
with doubled c-pawns.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.160
Black first connects knights and is now ready to question the bishop.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.200
White is no longer interested in meeting a6 with this capture, so he puts himself in position

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.360
to go elsewhere.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.960
White is timely on this move 5.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.680
Rook e1 is not just about centralizing a piece.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.440
f1 is now vacant so that the bishop can go back home.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.120
Its other options weren't appealing.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.000
Unstable, obstructs the center pawn, obstructs pieces.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.280
d5, e takes d, knight recaptures, the bishop is ready to play.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.000
Carlsen plays in the center.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.360
Move 8, d4.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.480
What are some of your thoughts when you see this move?

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.480
Feel free to pause the video, give it 30 seconds.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:32.040
Okay, I'll just be noting a few things for right now, and in a few moves I'll be highlighting

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.940
what I think the big picture idea is behind this advance.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.800
So some things you may say.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.960
There's tension between the pawns, and there's an intersection square on d4.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.160
Two defenders, two attackers.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.160
You may say white's trying to make things dynamic, open things up while his king is

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.320
cozy, and black's is in the center.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.040
And one last note, for now at least.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.000
This particular pawn exchange is inevitable.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.080
Let's get a few more moves in.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.800
The knight retreats so that the queen increases the pressure on d4.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.320
Neither side is so quick to release this tension.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.080
Each side wants one another to initiate the exchange so that they can recapture and improve

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.520
a piece.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.680
In comes bishop e3, more pressure on c5.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:36.120
White's not concerned about knight d5 or knight g4 bothering the bishop.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.460
A fine reply, a good reply in both cases, is bishop g5.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.920
In this game we have c takes d.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.360
Knight recapture, white has just improved a hair.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.600
Black puts himself in position to do similar, should white take on c6.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.600
Here we are on this move 11.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.480
I wonder if you can spot Carlsen's next move.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.880
What move would you play here?

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.800
What do you consider to be a healthy move?

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.000
Feel free to pause the video.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:22.720
Okay, an additional note behind move 8, d4, is that Carlsen was creating a queenside majority.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.960
We're working here with a 3 versus 2.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.840
He is not developing his queen knight to c3.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.920
He's taking into account this majority on this move 11.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.760
c4, it's in motion.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:44.080
A big square is taken away from the king knight, and next up he's ready to tuck the knight

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.360
right behind.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.840
If you're looking for a game that does an excellent job of highlighting a 3 to 2 queenside

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.040
majority, this is the one to have a closer look at.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.280
Really an excellent game.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.000
We continue.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:09.960
Knight takes knight, bishop takes knight, bishop c6, getting the minor pieces out.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:20.120
Prep work for the majority, denied a5, queen d3, new square for the queen rook.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:26.920
Also, white's looking to coordinate with the most menacing white piece, and that is

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:32.640
this bishop on d4, the one that points towards the heart of the kingside structure.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.840
Right around the corner, queen g3.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:45.760
Castles, rook a to d1, queen c7, bishop e5, queen b6, queen g3.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.300
The b-pawn, poison.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.720
In the game it is rook f to d8.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.480
If queen takes b2, we would have knight d5.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.920
Too many problems to solve.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.880
No good reply here.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.360
Alright, rook f to d8 in this game.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.040
The reply, rook takes rook.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.960
Bishop c7 is not a good idea.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.160
Going for this imbalance.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.920
It prefers black, two rooks outweighing the queen.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.760
Black has good coordination.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.680
That's the main thing you would look at in going for such an imbalance, being down the

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.160
queen.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.400
First we have the rook exchange.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.760
That variation I just highlighted helps us to understand why black does not recapture

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.520
with the rook here.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.880
Bishop c7 would hit.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.000
In this game it is queen takes rook.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:53.640
Carlsen gets to the open file, the only open file, and this is where black now goes wrong.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.880
In the game, queen b6 is played.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.960
Very easy, I would say, to go wrong.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.200
That seems very natural.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.240
What is considered best, queen f8.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.400
Safeguarding g7, and this frees up the knight.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:19.000
Ways to improve from here, h6, rook to d8, calls it around even.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:26.080
Now with queen b6, watch these next few moves closely.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:33.080
These next few moves allow Carlsen to advance his queenside majority.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:39.080
First, bishop d4, the queen better not go to the d file.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.560
Bishop takes knight.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.260
Queen's in trouble.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.260
She moves forward.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.960
This is really the best that black can do in this position.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:47.960
Queen b3.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:54.120
Seems like she's cozy there for the moment, but not quite.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.160
Rook d3 immediately questioning the queen.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.400
The knight is ready to jump.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.680
We have two pawns that are both poison.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.500
They're both indirectly defended.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.160
We know what happens on queen takes b2.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.320
What's happening in this position if queen takes c4?

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.760
In the game, the queen goes to c2.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:26.800
If queen takes c4 here, white can take on f6, and after the recapture there's a check,

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.000
and there goes the queen.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.840
So queen c2.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.680
Now we get to advance the majority, b4.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:47.440
These last two moves, bishop d4 and rook d3 have pulled the queen away from controlling

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.440
this b4 advance.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.960
b4 is available.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.680
You cannot take twice.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.800
a takes b, a takes b.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.880
Don't take on b4.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.560
You're going to lose the knight.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:08.560
Before you go, before Carlsen played b4, you really do have to factor some things in here,

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.760
because yes, you are getting your majority going.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:18.560
You're now working with a 2 to 1, but at the same time you have activated this rook.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.340
Are you okay with doing this?

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:28.660
Also are you okay with these last moves, bishop d4, rook d3, inviting the queen

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.180
this deep in your house?

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:37.980
Black cannot follow up by putting the rook on the first rank here.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:43.240
This is something that had to be weighed before playing b4.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.380
Why is rook a1 not a good move here?

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.660
He can capture on f6, this is one reason why.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:57.940
He can capture on f6, and after the recapture there's a back rank problem, mate, soon enough.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.740
So the black rook has a responsibility.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.260
The bishop has a responsibility.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.300
Where do we go from here?

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.540
It is knight h5.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:19.500
Now this next sequence, you have to see a few moves ahead.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:27.740
Carlsen saw that after queen e5, bishop f6, queen takes knight, bishop takes bishop, rook

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.860
takes bishop, and queen takes knight.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.860
He saw that in this position he has a very good move.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:40.180
How would you react to this position here?

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.420
How would you react to this threat on the rook?

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.420
Feel free to pause the video.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:53.860
Alright, are any of you tempted by action on the king side when the bishop and queen

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:54.860
was coordinated there?

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.060
Are we still focused on the king side?

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.940
Rook h4, is that a good way to go?

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:01.940
Computer says no.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:05.380
If you go with h6, you could even ignore the threat even.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:10.900
It says, be fine giving up this pawn and run away here.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:14.220
This goes nowhere if you form a battery on the h-file.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.660
What is absolute best?

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:22.660
The one that keeps white in a winning position.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:27.380
Queen a5, forget about the king side.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:32.280
There's a back rank weakness, and queen a5 takes advantage of it.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.420
We're not moving with the rook.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.820
We're attacking an unprotected piece and saying, I dare you, take my queen and it's going to

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.140
be mate in two.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:50.100
So this forces the rook off of the open file and it has to crawl up into a ball.

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:53.940
And now the queen is planted on b6.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.460
White's position is excellent.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.340
Look at the coordination.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:02.780
Better rook, better queen, everything is defended.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:10.540
The queen is in white's house, but not coordinated with any other black piece.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:13.300
Alright, where do we go from here?

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.260
e5.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:19.020
It does suggest rook to d8.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.700
In this game, it's rook d1.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:29.220
A flight square is produced, b5, the majority continues to roll.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:36.780
And the follow up here, Carlsen puts the queen on f6, and this renews back rank mating ideas.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:40.680
He takes away that flight square from black.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:48.860
What is considered better is to get a flight square in and then put the queen on c7.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:56.900
It suggests keeping an eye on these two pawns and next looking to advance with c5.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:12:01.940
So some advantage, a bit of an advantage, is given away here with this move.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:07.860
As appealing as it looks, planting the queen in this hole on f6.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:14.020
From here, h5, h4, each side, well white has a flight square now.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:17.340
Black has an additional flight square.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:24.500
Bishop f5, rook d5, the e-pawn falls.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.780
But black now has good pieces all of a sudden.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:36.580
The bishop is on e6, and the rook is able to activate right around the corner for black.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:43.060
We have this increased pressure on the pinned bishop.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:46.860
How to get out of this pin?

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:53.380
Retreat, queen e2, next up there's rook to d1.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:58.940
Black does not have time to form a battery on the back rank.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:08.500
If rook a1 in this position, there would follow rook check, king h7, and queen e5.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:13.820
White can afford to give up this bishop with check.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:20.420
Because in this position right here, there's no good way to get at the king.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:26.740
This check is covered by the queen, and there's a big problem on h8.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:30.420
This bishop does not help defend against this threat.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:36.300
f6, there's queen c7, leading to mate in a couple.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:39.780
So not so fast, rook.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.220
Instead we have king h7.

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:47.620
This now gives white time to unpin the bishop.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:55.220
Now that it's unpinned, the queen gets back on the offensive, targets the b-pawn.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:14:00.180
In this position, rook a1 is played.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:04.780
From here on out, it's considered winning for white.

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:14.540
The only move to stay in the game apparently, not very human at all, is rook a7.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:20.060
If you play rook a7, you have to know you could be kicked.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.140
Computer says this is still even-ish.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:32.500
White just has a small advantage with rook a2, queen takes b7, rook b2, and now there

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:37.340
are these ideas of bishop g4.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:42.980
If the rook leaves the back rank, black gets there.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:49.460
If the rook leaves defensive d4, the queen could go here, targeting f2.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:55.000
Not at all easy to see a variation like this.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.240
In this one, it is rook a1.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:03.520
We get a rook exchange, and from here, some calculation.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:12.640
We first get going with the pawns, and after queen c3, he can take on b2.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:21.000
Black is going to win this bishop, but these pawns are very dangerous.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.200
We have queen e1.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:27.360
This stops the queen from getting to e4.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.440
If the queen could get to e4, that would stop bishop c4.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:36.780
So we've got a pin and defense of e4.

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:39.360
He continues to advance.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.480
If some pin like this is here, black simply gets out of it.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.480
Queen b1, and then next up, bishop c4.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.760
Soon this bishop will be tracked.

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.080
In the game, it is b6.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:55.200
He advances the more dangerous passed pawn.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.600
I consider the b-pawn more dangerous because this promotion square, a dark square, this

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:05.600
guy cannot defend that promotion square.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:09.920
So this is the faster pawn.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:12.200
From here, bishop c4.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.320
He has it all calculated.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:15.800
Queen f3.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:23.960
He is okay losing a piece with check because these guys are just too good.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:36.880
Queen b1, b7, queen b5, c6, bishop d5, and just one more move, queen g3.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.400
Black resigns.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:40.800
Everything is covered.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.840
g2, even if there was coordinated pressure on that, no problem.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:47.600
Queen's covering.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:49.480
Everything is glued together.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:50.840
King is cozy.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:53.360
Queen from g3 supports.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:56.240
b8 equals queen next.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:00.720
No good reply here for black.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.880
What did you think of this one?

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:09.080
Anyhow, feel free, as usual, to leave any feedback to this video in the comments section

00:17:09.080 --> 00:17:10.080
below.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:14.240
I hope you enjoyed it and maybe took a thing or two away.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:15.240
That's all for now.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:16.240
Take care.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:16.740
Bye.

